<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crocus (Alemannic king)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 306</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Crocus was an Alemannic king in Roman service in the early fourth century. According to the late Roman Epitome de Caesaribus, he accompanied the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and helped bring about the proclamation of Constantius's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> as emperor at York in 306. His career shows how Germanic leaders and their followers served in the late Roman army.</p></div>
